//// Decision 63 (amended 2026-09-19) — the TUPLE is the one receiver the
//// ambient `Index<K, V>` behavior cannot express, and the amendment says so
//// outright: `t[0]` needs a **constant** index and answers a type **per
//// position**, which `at(key: K) -> ?V` cannot say with a single `V`. So the
//// tuple stays the checker's special case.
////
//// What the special case costs is a checker arm and no backend arm: `t[0]` is
//// rewritten to the positional member access the language already has and all
//// four targets already emit (`t._0`), so the element's type is the one its
//// position declares — `i32` here, `string` there, neither of them optional,
//// because nothing about a tuple position is absent.
////
//// The two refusals are `reject/index_tuple_computed.bp` (a computed index)
//// and `reject/index_tuple_out_of_range.bp` (a position the type has not got).

pub fn main() {
    val t = #(1, "a");
    val n: i32 = t[0];
    val s: string = t[1];
    @print(n);
    @print(s);

    val pair: #(x: i32, y: i32) = #(10, 20);
    @print(pair[0] + pair[1]);
}
